  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a density-based optimization method inspired by M.C. Escher's periodic patterns to construct block structures that minimize overlap. Unlike traditional optimization methods, this approach leverages mathematical properties of periodic functions to identify structures with minimal correlation. The key innovation is the use of random amplitude perturbations to escape local optima while maintaining a mathematical structure that can be analyzed. The bottleneck addressed is the search space limitation common to gradient-based optimization, which often becomes trapped in high-value local regions. We assume the optimal solution has a periodic component and separates high and low density regions, allowing us to systematically explore promising regions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: A periodic block function h(x) = \u03a3 a_i * sin(2\u03c0 \u03bd_i x), constrained to [0,1] and with total integral 1.\n- Variables: Four parameters (a,b,c,d) defining block sizes and phases, along with frequency \u03bd, amplitude A, and phase \u03b8 for the sine components.\n- Objective: Minimize C5 by first finding amplitudes that decorrelate periods with high variance, then training a vector quantizer.\n- Constraints: Sum(h_i) = n/2 (dx=2.0/n), 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1.\n- Derived formulas: Variance analysis for overlap reduction, Fourier periodicity constraints.\n- Iteration: (1) Subspace initialization, (2) Amplitude perturbation, (3) Phase-gradient descent, (4) Density adjustment with canonical duality.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize seeded random positions: \n   - mesh = [(i/100 - seed) % 1 for i in range(2*n)]\n   - bins = selection\n   Phase adjustment mechanism:\n      rewrite objective using nuclear norms\n      dualize the displacement constraints\n      iteratively project gradient updates into sparse manifold\n   Score calculation:\n      corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n      c5 = max(corr) * dx\n   Density repair:\n      apply Malmquist stabilizer\n      enforce binary constraint via hard-threshold\n   Laplacian regularization:\n      penalize neighbors too similar\n      add prior for periodicity\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Default template: single-frequency response with 12 harmonics\n   - sampling_rate = 16, \n   - basis = mixed signals\n   - decay = 0.8\n   Sharp variations include:\n     - Frequency-push: increase \u03bd by 1.0 at 20% mark\n     - Gradient-escape: add anti-gradient noise when improvement stalls\n     - Canonical change: alternate between sine and exponential steps\n   Trigger rules:\n     - Stop on convergence (duhamel product < \u03b5)\n     - Exit after 80% budget if no improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nPhases:\n  Stage1: 20% of budget - density calibration (n_vectors=100)\n  Stage2: 50% budget - phase-space descent\n  Stage3: 30% budget - post-processing refinement\nResource-allocation:\n  - Vector-store: maintain 2^10 = 1024 density patterns\n  - Memory management: deprecated prints retained for trajectory\n  - Timeout triggers: kill process if score diverges\nFallback procedure:\n  if invalid: \n      extract silhouette + gradient features\n      recompute via cross-validated basis\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Compute C5 on candidate: np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * dx\n- Verify 0<=value<=1\n- Package result: (h_final, score, n)\n- Include density certification: \n  hbars = np.bincount(np.rint(h)); \n  assert all(x>=0 and x<=1 for x in hbars)\n- Output must satisfy:\n  return (h, score, n)\n  h evaluated using np.correlate\n  score is finite",
